For reading we have to read this passage here and&nbsp;
then answer questions about it. In the passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.080
there's a part where a teacher is telling&nbsp;
her students about a trip that she went on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.600
and this is described from the point of view&nbsp;
of one of her students. Question 10 asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.040
which sentence is the best evidence that the&nbsp;
students are baffled by the teacher's story.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.320
"And can you guess what we saw&nbsp;
from the train" Miss Spivy asked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.760
We could not. "Camels!" she said.&nbsp;
"We saw a whole caravan of camels."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.600
She looked around the room waiting for us&nbsp;
to be amazed and delighted at the thought.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.440
I thought that last sentence was the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.840
but I got it wrong it's actually&nbsp;
the sentence that comes next.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.560
We all hung there for a minute thinking&nbsp;
hard until Mavis Davis spoke up.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.640
So that's the reading section let's&nbsp;
have a look at the writing one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.320
You've also got a passage to read but then it's&nbsp;
marked with little numbers for each question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.560
where it will ask you if there's a&nbsp;
better way to say what is underlined.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.880
With the math section at least it doesn't&nbsp;
seem impossible that if you kept trying you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.680
might be able to get close to a perfect score&nbsp;
and that's the interesting thing its that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:33.600
can actually take this test I believe as many&nbsp;
times as you want so you can keep taking it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.800
until you get a score that you're happy with.&nbsp;
It's offered seven times throughout the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.840
but the thing with that is that it costs&nbsp;
money to take, it costs around fifty dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.920
which does certainly sound unfair to people&nbsp;
who can't afford to just keep taking it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.760
then there's the cost of things like tutors&nbsp;
and other preparation to try and do better.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:02.240
So the SAT might seem kinda easy and it's probably&nbsp;
not good at distinguishing between the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:08.640
students because there are no extremely difficult&nbsp;
problems. The makers of the test claim that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:15.200
SAT does assess the literacy and numeracy skills&nbsp;
that a student would need to succeed in college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.200
and they also claim that the SAT combined&nbsp;
with high school grades is a better indicator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.800
of success in college than high school&nbsp;
grades alone but more and more colleges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:34.400
are making the SAT optional on your application&nbsp;
and it all points towards a more holistic approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.000
to college applications. The top schools&nbsp;
are still very competitive but you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.760
to find other ways to stand out rather than&nbsp;
just performing well on a standardized test.

